summary:
- Every mapping that follows a carriage return in the printed output lands on the wrong generated line, in both `bun build --sourcemap` output and the maps the runtime uses to remap `error.stack`: a lone `` followed by a one-character line is not counted as a line break, and an actual `
` is counted as two. - Repro: bundle `/*! ab
c */
console.log(1);
throw new Error("x");
` with `--sourcemap=external`. The output puts `console.log` on its 5th line (the `` ends a line, as it does fo

- `position.lineText` of a CSS `BuildMessage` (a parse or minify error from `Bun.build` / `bun build`) is not the text of the reported line: - on line 2 and later it starts with the `
` that ended the previous line, and in LF files it also ends with its own `
`: `"
@import "x";
"` instead of `"@import "x";"` - on the last line of a file without a trailing line break it is `""`, and `bun build` prints no code frame for the error - in a file with lone `` or form feed line breaks it is th

- A negative `lineOffset` is ignored at runtime by `vm.Script`, `vm.runInThisContext` and `vm.runInContext`. `new vm.Script("1;
2;
3;
throw new Error('x')", { filename: "n.js", lineOffset: -2 }).runInThisContext()` produces a stack starting `n.js:4` / `Error: x` / `at n.js:4:16`; Node prints `n.js:2`, the source line, a caret, then `at n.js:2:7`. Positive offsets work. A negative offset is the usual way to compensate for wrapper lines a caller prepended to the source. - The header node:vm pre

- `vm.compileFunction("#!/usr/bin/env node
return 1", [])` throws `Error: Failed to compile function`. Node returns a function. - Same for the CommonJS shape, `vm.compileFunction("#!...
module.exports = 1", ["exports", "require", "module", ...])`, which is how loaders built on `compileFunction` compile files that start with a shebang. - Cause: `stringifyAnonymousFunction` (`src/jsc/bindings/NodeVM.cpp`) compiles the body as the program `(function (<params>) {
<body>
})`. Inside that wrapper

